WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.000
Underneath sit two structures:
privilege and applications.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.743
For privilege, use the
least-privileged role for each task,

3
00:00:07.743 --> 00:00:12.500
and keep fewer than five Global
Administrators, as Microsoft recommends.

4
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:17.342
Use role-assignable groups, and tier
everything along Microsoft's enterprise access

5
00:00:17.342 --> 00:00:21.912
model, which builds on the old Active
Directory tiers rather than replacing them.

6
00:00:21.912 --> 00:00:25.932
For applications, the surprise is
that app owners hold real admin

7
00:00:25.932 --> 00:00:30.175
rights over their app, including
adding a credential, so keep them few.

8
00:00:30.175 --> 00:00:33.033
Admin consent goes through
a governed workflow,

9
00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:38.250
and apps authenticate with certificates or
federated credentials instead of secrets.

10
00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:42.057
And here's what people get wrong:
consent grants never expire,

11
00:00:42.057 --> 00:00:45.787
and revoking one leaves tokens
already issued still valid.

12
00:00:45.787 --> 00:00:49.688
So application access needs the
same review discipline as people.

13
00:00:49.688 --> 00:00:51.340
Next, who decides.
